{"subjectId":"sql","id":"constraints","title":"Constraints","group":"Defining & Changing Data","order":7,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Constraints are rules the database enforces on every insert and update. They keep bad data out no matter which application, script or person writes it.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Constraint</th><th>Guarantees</th><th>Sample database</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>PRIMARY KEY</code></td><td>Unique <strong>and</strong> not null; identifies each row</td><td><code>employees.emp_id</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>FOREIGN KEY</code></td><td>Value must exist in the referenced table (or be <code>NULL</code>)</td><td><code>employees.dept_id → departments</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>UNIQUE</code></td><td>No two rows share the value (<code>NULL</code>s allowed)</td><td><code>employees.email</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>NOT NULL</code></td><td>A value must be supplied</td><td><code>employees.name</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>CHECK</code></td><td>A boolean condition must not be false</td><td><code>salary &gt; 0</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>DEFAULT</code></td><td>Value used when the column is omitted</td><td><code>status DEFAULT 'pending'</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-ban\"></i>Each one rejecting bad data</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL — NOT NULL</figcaption><pre><code>INSERT INTO employees (emp_id, name, dept_id, salary, hire_date)\nVALUES (11, NULL, 1, 50000, '2024-06-01');</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Error</figcaption><pre><code>NOT NULL constraint failed: employees.name</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL — UNIQUE</figcaption><pre><code>INSERT INTO employees (emp_id, name, email, dept_id, salary, hire_date)\nVALUES (11, 'Tara', 'asha@corp.in', 1, 50000, '2024-06-01');</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Error</figcaption><pre><code>UNIQUE constraint failed: employees.email</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL — CHECK</figcaption><pre><code>UPDATE employees SET salary = -100 WHERE emp_id = 3;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Error</figcaption><pre><code>CHECK constraint failed: salary &gt; 0</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL — FOREIGN KEY (child side)</figcaption><pre><code>INSERT INTO employees (emp_id, name, dept_id, salary, hire_date)\nVALUES (11, 'Tara', 99, 50000, '2024-06-01');     -- there is no department 99</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Error</figcaption><pre><code>FOREIGN KEY constraint failed</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL — FOREIGN KEY (parent side)</figcaption><pre><code>DELETE FROM departments WHERE dept_id = 1;         -- Engineering still has employees</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Error</figcaption><pre><code>FOREIGN KEY constraint failed</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL — PRIMARY KEY</figcaption><pre><code>INSERT INTO employees (emp_id, name, dept_id, salary, hire_date)\nVALUES (1, 'Tara', 1, 50000, '2024-06-01');       -- emp_id 1 is Asha</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Error</figcaption><pre><code>UNIQUE constraint failed: employees.emp_id</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>UNIQUE</code> still allows several <code>NULL</code>s — Neha and Dev both have a <code>NULL</code> email. <code>NULL</code> means <em>unknown</em>, and two unknowns are not considered equal. (SQL Server is the exception: it allows only one <code>NULL</code> in a unique column unless you use a filtered index.)</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-tag\"></i>Named constraints, composite keys, defaults</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>CREATE TABLE enrollments (\n    student_id  INTEGER NOT NULL,\n    course_id   INTEGER NOT NULL,\n    grade       CHAR(1),\n    enrolled_on TEXT    NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_DATE,\n    status      TEXT    NOT NULL DEFAULT 'active',\n    CONSTRAINT pk_enrollment PRIMARY KEY (student_id, course_id),\n    CONSTRAINT chk_grade CHECK (grade IN ('A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'F'))\n);\nINSERT INTO enrollments (student_id, course_id) VALUES (1, 10);\nSELECT student_id, course_id, grade, status,\n       enrolled_on = CURRENT_DATE AS enrolled_today\nFROM enrollments;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>student_id | course_id | grade | status | enrolled_today\n-----------+-----------+-------+--------+---------------\n1          | 10        | NULL  | active | 1</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>INSERT INTO enrollments (student_id, course_id, grade) VALUES (1, 11, 'Z');</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Error</figcaption><pre><code>CHECK constraint failed: chk_grade</code></pre></figure>\n<p>A <strong>named</strong> constraint shows up in the error (<code>chk_grade</code>), which makes failures much easier to diagnose — and you need the name to drop it later (<code>ALTER TABLE … DROP CONSTRAINT chk_grade</code>). Notice too that the first row's <code>NULL</code> grade passed the <code>CHECK</code>: a check only rejects a condition that is <strong>false</strong>, and <code>NULL IN (…)</code> is <em>unknown</em>. Add <code>NOT NULL</code> if the value is mandatory.</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-git-fork\"></i>Referential actions: what happens to children?</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><code>ON DELETE …</code></th><th>When the parent row is deleted</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>RESTRICT</code> / <code>NO ACTION</code> (default)</td><td>Refuse the delete while children exist</td></tr><tr><td><code>CASCADE</code></td><td>Delete the children too</td></tr><tr><td><code>SET NULL</code></td><td>Set the child's foreign key to <code>NULL</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>SET DEFAULT</code></td><td>Set it to the column default</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON;\nCREATE TABLE authors (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT);\nCREATE TABLE posts (\n    id        INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n    author_id INTEGER REFERENCES authors(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n    title     TEXT\n);\nCREATE TABLE comments (\n    id        INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n    author_id INTEGER REFERENCES authors(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,\n    body      TEXT\n);\nINSERT INTO authors  VALUES (1, 'Asha'), (2, 'Ravi');\nINSERT INTO posts    VALUES (1, 1, 'Intro to SQL'), (2, 2, 'Joins'), (3, 1, 'Indexes');\nINSERT INTO comments VALUES (1, 1, 'Nice!'), (2, 2, 'Thanks');\n\nDELETE FROM authors WHERE id = 1;\n\nSELECT * FROM posts ORDER BY id;\nSELECT * FROM comments ORDER BY id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>id | author_id | title\n---+-----------+------\n2  | 2         | Joins\n\nid | author_id | body\n---+-----------+-------\n1  | NULL      | Nice!\n2  | 2         | Thanks</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Use <code>CASCADE</code> deliberately: deleting one customer could silently remove years of orders. Many teams prefer <code>RESTRICT</code> plus soft deletes (<code>is_active = 0</code>).</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between PRIMARY KEY and UNIQUE?</summary><div><p>Both enforce uniqueness and create an index. A table has <strong>one</strong> primary key and it can never be <code>NULL</code>; it is the row's identity and the target of foreign keys. A table can have <strong>many</strong> <code>UNIQUE</code> constraints and they allow <code>NULL</code>s (multiple, in most engines).</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Can a foreign key column be NULL?</summary><div><p>Yes, unless it is also <code>NOT NULL</code>. A <code>NULL</code> foreign key means “no related row” (e.g. Dev has no department). The constraint is only checked for non-null values.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why enforce constraints in the database if the application validates input?</summary><div><p>Several applications, scripts, admins and migrations write to the same database; bugs and race conditions happen (two requests inserting the same email at once). Only the database sees every write and can guarantee integrity atomically.</p></div></details></section>"}